window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"][5]={"university_biology_en:M5-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M5-L1 – Amino acids and protein structure<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Biochemistry and Bioenergetics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L3<\/a>: functional groups; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L6<\/a>: acid–base equilibria;\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L3<\/a>: compartments.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Classify amino acids by\nside-chain properties, explain levels of protein structure and estimate\nthe pH-dependent charge of a simple model amino acid.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A protein is not a rigid building block. Its amino acid sequence and\nenvironment jointly determine the accessible conformations. Spatial\norganization affects binding, catalysis, transport and mechanical\nproperties.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"amino-acids-and-peptide-bonds\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Amino acids and peptide\nbonds<\/h3>\n<p>Proteinogenic α-amino acids have a central carbon atom attached to an\namino group, a carboxyl group, hydrogen and a variable side chain R.\nGlycine is achiral; most amino acids used in ribosomal protein synthesis\nhave the L configuration. Nonpolar side chains such as valine often\nfavour sheltered regions of water-soluble proteins. Serine has a polar\nhydroxyl group; aspartate and lysine have negatively and positively\ncharged side chains, respectively, under many physiological conditions.\nThese classifications depend on local pH and environment.<\/p>\n<p>A peptide bond is an amide bond between a carboxyl and an amino\ngroup. Formally, linkage involves the removal of water; ribosomal\nsynthesis uses energetically activated aminoacyl-tRNAs. Partial\ndouble-bond character makes the peptide group approximately planar.\nSequences are read from the N terminus to the C terminus. Free terminal\ngroups and ionizable side chains together determine net charge.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"levels-of-structure-and-stability\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Levels of structure and\nstability<\/h3>\n<p>Primary structure is the sequence; secondary structure describes\nlocal backbone arrangements such as α-helices and β-sheets, whose\nhydrogen bonds typically link peptide groups. Tertiary structure\ndescribes the spatial organization of a polypeptide chain; quaternary\nstructure results from association of multiple chains. Not every protein\nhas a stable global fold or multiple subunits. The hydrophobic effect,\nhydrogen bonds, electrostatic interactions, van der Waals contacts and\nsometimes disulfide bonds contribute to stability. Chaperones assist\nfolding without rewriting the amino acid sequence.<\/p>\n<p>Denaturation alters the native structure, for instance through heat\nor extreme pH; peptide bonds often remain intact. Aggregation can\nprevent refolding. A sequence change can therefore be neutral, alter\nstability or affect a binding site; the substitution alone does not\nimply a general loss of function.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_023_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic sequence, local helix, folded single chain and two associated subunits; not a real protein structure.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic sequence, local helix, folded\nsingle chain and two associated subunits; not a real protein\nstructure.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Schematic sequence, local helix, folded single chain and\ntwo associated subunits; not a real protein structure.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>For free glycine in an idealized model, let the pKₐ values be 2.34\n(carboxyl group) and 9.60 (ammonium group). At pH 7 the carboxyl group\nis predominantly deprotonated and the amino group predominantly\nprotonated: the zwitterion dominates. The isoelectric point is\napproximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2.34<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>9.60<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.97<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2.34+9.60)/2=5.97<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt this point the mean net charge, not the number of charges, is zero.\nAbove this point the mean charge is more negative. In proteins, local\ninteractions can shift pKₐ values.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the isoelectric point of a free amino acid without an\nionizable side chain, with pKₐ values of 2.2 and 9.8.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which structural level distinguishes two proteins with identical\nsequences but different spatial arrangements of their single\nchains?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Explain why denaturation does not automatically imply hydrolysis of\nall peptide bonds.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2.2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>9.8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2.2+9.8)/2=6.0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Tertiary structure; local differences may also affect secondary\nstructure.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The native fold largely depends on interactions that can change\nwithout cleavage of the covalent backbone.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The pI averaging rule applies here only to a simple system with two\nrelevant ionizable groups. Further groups require a net-charge balance.\nThe figure shows structural levels schematically, not a real protein\nstructure. Not every helix is an α-helix, and not every mutation has a\ndetectable effect.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Amino acid chemistry links sequence, spatial organization and\nfunction. M5-L2 explores how proteins and other biocatalysts affect\nreaction rates.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-4-proteins\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 3.4 Proteins<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Amino acids, peptide bonds and levels\nof protein structure · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M5-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M5-L2 – Enzyme kinetics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Biochemistry and Bioenergetics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L2<\/a>: equilibrium and free\nenergy; <a href=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L1<\/a>: protein\nstructure; <a href=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>:\nfunctions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish enzyme catalysis\nfrom reaction thermodynamics, calculate initial velocities using\nMichaelis–Menten kinetics and compare inhibition types through parameter\nchanges.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Many thermodynamically possible reactions are too slow for a living\norganism without catalysis. Enzymes accelerate specific reactions by\nproviding a pathway with a lower activation barrier. Catalytic RNA also\nexists. A catalyst does not shift thermodynamic equilibrium.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"steady-state-model\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Steady-state model<\/h3>\n<p>The simple scheme\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo>⇌<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E+S\\rightleftharpoons ES\\rightarrow E+P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes enzyme E, substrate S, the complex ES and product P. For\ninitial velocities with negligible reverse reaction from product,\nsubstrate excess and approximately steady-state ES,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_0=\\frac{V_{\\max}[S]}{K_m+[S]},\\qquad V_{\\max}=k_{cat}[E]_T.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas concentration units and is the substrate concentration at half the\nmaximum velocity in this model.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_{cat}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the maximum turnover rate per active enzyme unit (inverse time).\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_m=(k_{-1}+k_{cat})/k_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis generally not the dissociation constant. At low substrate\nconcentrations\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis approximately proportional to [S]; at very high concentrations the\nrate approaches\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{\\max}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe slope near the origin is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{\\max}/K_m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"inhibition-and-regulation\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Inhibition and regulation<\/h3>\n<p>In ideal reversible competitive inhibition, an inhibitor competes\nwith S for binding to free E. Apparent\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincreases by the factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=1+[I]/K_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{\\max}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis unchanged. Pure noncompetitive inhibition decreases\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{\\max}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith unchanged\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nmixed inhibition changes both. In the ideal model, uncompetitive\ninhibition binds only ES and reduces both parameters by the same factor.\nAllosteric enzymes may display sigmoidal rather than hyperbolic curves.\nCofactors, pH, temperature and reversible modifications affect catalytic\nactivity.<\/p>\n<p>Parameter estimation benefits from multiple substrate concentrations,\nreplicates, controlled conditions and direct nonlinear fitting of\ninitial rates. Reciprocal transformations can distort the error\nstructure. Parameter differences alone are not conclusive evidence for a\nmolecular inhibition mechanism.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_024_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Simulated saturation curves with Vmax 12 µmol/min and Km 2 or 6 mM; at 6 mM the rates are 9 and 6 µmol/min.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Simulated saturation curves with Vmax 12\nµmol/min and Km 2 or 6 mM; at 6 mM the rates are 9 and 6\nµmol/min.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Simulated saturation curves with Vmax 12 µmol/min and Km\n2 or 6 mM; at 6 mM the rates are 9 and 6 µmol/min.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>Simulated enzyme:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{\\max}=12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµmol/min and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_m=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmM. At [S] = 2 mM, the initial velocity is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\cdot2/(2+2)=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµmol/min; at 6 mM it is 9 µmol/min. With ideal competitive inhibition\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_{m,app}=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmM. At 6 mM the velocity is now 6 µmol/min. The figure displays both\ntheoretical curves.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the uninhibited velocity at [S] = 10 mM.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the velocity at [S] = 2 mM under the same competitive\ninhibition?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to the equilibrium of a reversible reaction when a\ncatalyst is added?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\cdot10/12=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµmol/min.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\cdot2/8=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµmol/min.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It remains unchanged under the same thermodynamic conditions;\nforward and reverse reactions are accelerated.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The model assumes a simple reaction, stable enzyme amount and defined\ninitial conditions. Substrate or product inhibition, multiple\nsubstrates, cooperativity or inactivation may cause deviations. The\ncurves are simulated; they are not experimental measurements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Enzymes control metabolic flux through specific reactions. M5-L3\nintroduces carbohydrates and lipids as major substrates, stores and\nstructural materials.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L3<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-5-enzymes\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 6.5 Enzymes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Catalysis, regulation and cofactors ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK554481/?report=classic\">StatPearls\n– Biochemistry, Proteins Enzymes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Michaelis–Menten kinetics and\ntypes of inhibition · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M5-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M5-L3 – Carbohydrates and lipids<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Biochemistry and Bioenergetics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L3<\/a>: organic groups; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L2<\/a>: membrane\nstructure.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish carbohydrate and\nlipid classes by linkages, polarity and function and calculate a\ncondensation balance for a linear polysaccharide.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Starch and cellulose both consist of glucose units but have very\ndifferent material properties. The spatial configuration of their\nlinkages helps determine structure, degradability and biological\nfunction. Lipids likewise do not form a single polymer class.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"carbohydrates\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Carbohydrates<\/h3>\n<p>Monosaccharides are polyhydroxy aldehydes or ketones or their\nderivatives. Many sugars form rings in water. Ring closure of glucose\nproduces an anomeric centre; α- and β-glucose differ in configuration at\nthis centre. A glycosidic bond links a sugar through its anomeric centre\nto an appropriate group on a partner. The notation 1→4 identifies the\nparticipating carbon atoms.<\/p>\n<p>Amylose has predominantly α(1→4) linkages; amylopectin and glycogen\nalso have α(1→6) branches. Cellulose consists of β(1→4)-linked glucose\nunits. Aligned chains form stable fibrous assemblies. Many animals\ncannot cleave cellulose with their own digestive enzymes, although some\nuse symbiotic microorganisms. Branched storage polymers provide numerous\nsites for degrading enzymes. Sugar chains also serve molecular\nrecognition and form components of glycoproteins and glycolipids.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"lipids\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Lipids<\/h3>\n<p>Fatty acids have a carboxyl group and a hydrocarbon chain. Saturated\nchains have no C=C double bonds; cis double bonds can disrupt regular\npacking. Triacylglycerols consist of glycerol and three fatty acids\nattached through ester bonds. They are hydrophobic energy stores.\nGlycerophospholipids typically possess two hydrophobic chains and a\npolar phosphate-containing head group. This amphiphilicity allows\nmembrane structures to form in water. Sterols have a ring system and\ninfluence membrane organization, among other functions. Not every lipid\ncontains fatty acids.<\/p>\n<p>Due to their high degree of reduction, lipids typically yield more\nenergy per mass upon complete oxidation than carbohydrates. Hydrophobic\nstores also associate with little water. Biological availability and\nactual energy turnover nevertheless depend on degradation pathways and\nphysiological state.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_025_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic comparison of three fatty acid chains in triacylglycerol and two chains plus a polar head group in glycerophospholipid.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic comparison of three fatty acid\nchains in triacylglycerol and two chains plus a polar head group in\nglycerophospholipid.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Schematic comparison of three fatty acid chains in\ntriacylglycerol and two chains plus a polar head group in\nglycerophospholipid.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>A linear unbranched model polysaccharide consisting of 100 glucose\nunits has 99 glycosidic bonds. In the formal condensation balance, 99\nwater molecules are removed. With rounded molar masses of 180 g/mol for\nglucose and 18 g/mol for water, the model molecule has a molar mass of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>180<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>99<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>18<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16218<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\cdot180-99\\cdot18=16218<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ng/mol. Biological synthesis usually uses activated sugars as building\nblocks; the formal balance does not describe the enzymatic synthesis\npathway.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many bonds and what formal molar mass does the corresponding\nlinear model of ten glucose units have?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which linkage distinguishes cellulose from amylose?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why do triacylglycerols not form the same typical bilayer as\nglycerophospholipids?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Nine bonds;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>180<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>18<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1638<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\cdot180-9\\cdot18=1638<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ng/mol.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">β(1→4) instead of predominantly α(1→4).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Triacylglycerols lack a pronounced polar head group; they tend to\nassemble as hydrophobic storage droplets.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The molar masses are rounded teaching values for a defined unmodified\nmolecule. Natural polymers often exhibit a distribution of chain\nlengths. The schematic lipid figure is not a complete chemical\nstructural formula; chain length, head group and double-bond position\nvary.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Carbohydrates and lipids link structure with storage and metabolism.\nM5-L4 follows glucose degradation and its coupling to ATP synthesis.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L4<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-2-carbohydrates\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 3.2 Carbohydrates<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Monosaccharides and glycosidic\nbonds; polysaccharides · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids\">OpenStax –\nBiology 2e, 3.3 Lipids<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Fatty acids, triacylglycerols,\nphospholipids and steroids · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M5-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M5-L4 – Glycolysis, citric acid cycle and respiratory chain<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Biochemistry and Bioenergetics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L2<\/a>: free energy; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L2<\/a>: electrochemical\ngradients; <a href=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L2<\/a>:\nenzymes.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish carbon, electron and\nATP balances in aerobic glucose degradation, explain chemiosmotic\ncoupling and calculate a model ATP yield.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>During degradation of organic molecules, chemical free energy is\ndistributed among several carriers. NADH and other reduced cofactors\ntransfer electrons; ATP couples exergonic and endergonic processes.\nEnergy transfer proceeds in steps rather than in a single combustion\nreaction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"glycolysis-and-the-citric-acid-cycle\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Glycolysis and the citric\nacid cycle<\/h3>\n<p>In the cytosol, one glucose (6 C) is converted to two pyruvates (3 C\neach). Hexokinase and phosphofructokinase initially consume two ATP.\nCleavage of a hexose phosphate provides two triose phosphates for the\npayoff phase, which produces four ATP by substrate-level phosphorylation\nand two NADH in total. The net yield per glucose is two ATP and two\nNADH. Glycolysis does not directly require molecular oxygen. When\nrespiratory regeneration of NAD⁺ is insufficient, fermentation (such as\nlactate formation) can reoxidize NADH and sustain glycolysis.<\/p>\n<p>In many eukaryotes pyruvate undergoes oxidative decarboxylation in\nthe mitochondrial matrix: each pyruvate yields acetyl-CoA (2 C), CO₂ and\nNADH. The acetyl group condenses with oxaloacetate (4 C) to form citrate\n(6 C). Oxidations and two decarboxylations occur during the citric acid\ncycle; oxaloacetate is regenerated. Each acetyl-CoA yields three NADH,\none FADH₂ equivalent (bound to succinate dehydrogenase), one GTP or ATP\nand two CO₂. The carbon atoms released immediately as CO₂ need not be\nthe newly introduced acetyl carbons during the first turn.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"electron-transport-and-coupling\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Electron transport and\ncoupling<\/h3>\n<p>NADH transfers electrons to complex I; succinate dehydrogenase\n(complex II) feeds electrons into the ubiquinone pool. Via complex III,\ncytochrome c and complex IV they ultimately reach oxygen, which is\nreduced to water. I, III and IV contribute to proton transport across\nthe inner mitochondrial membrane, whereas II does not. Proton flow back\ninto the matrix through ATP synthase drives ATP formation from ADP and\nphosphate. Proton-motive force includes electrical and chemical\ncomponents. Uncoupling can separate electron transport from ATP\nsynthesis and release energy as heat.<\/p>\n<p>Metabolic flux is regulated by substrate supply, redox state and\nenergy demand. Phosphofructokinase, for example, responds to energy\ncarriers and other metabolites. The citric acid cycle also supplies\nbiosynthetic precursors; withdrawal requires replenishment through\nanaplerotic reactions.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_026_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Balance per glucose: glycolysis, pyruvate oxidation and the citric acid cycle together yield 6 CO₂, 4 ATP/GTP, 10 NADH and 2 FADH₂ equivalents; reduced carriers feed the respiratory chain.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Balance per glucose: glycolysis, pyruvate\noxidation and the citric acid cycle together yield 6 CO₂, 4 ATP/GTP, 10\nNADH and 2 FADH₂ equivalents; reduced carriers feed the respiratory\nchain.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Balance per glucose: glycolysis, pyruvate oxidation and\nthe citric acid cycle together yield 6 CO₂, 4 ATP/GTP, 10 NADH and 2\nFADH₂ equivalents; reduced carriers feed the respiratory chain.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>For one completely oxidized glucose, the simplified balance model\nyields a total of 4 ATP/GTP by substrate-level phosphorylation, 10 NADH\n(including 2 cytosolic NADH) and 2 FADH₂ equivalents. Using model values\nof 2.5 ATP per NADH and 1.5 per FADH₂ equivalent yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>32<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4+10\\cdot2.5+2\\cdot1.5=32<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nATP equivalents. If transfer of electrons from the two cytosolic NADH\ninstead yields 1.5 ATP each, the total is 30. These are rounded model\nvalues, not universal cellular measurements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many CO₂ are accounted for when one glucose is completely\ndegraded through pyruvate oxidation and the citric acid cycle?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate ATP equivalents for 4 ATP/GTP, 8 NADH at 2.5 each and 4\nelectron pairs yielding 1.5 ATP each.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does oxygen deprivation inhibit oxidative phosphorylation even\nthough oxygen is not a direct substrate of glycolysis?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two from pyruvate oxidation and four from the citric acid cycle, six\nin total.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4+8\\cdot2.5+4\\cdot1.5=30<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Without the terminal electron acceptor the mitochondrial electron\ntransport chain stalls; generation of the proton gradient and\nrespiratory reoxidation of reduced cofactors are restricted.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The balance assumes complete aerobic oxidation and idealized coupling\nratios. Proton leak, transport costs, shuttle systems, biosynthesis and\nthe organism affect actual yields. Prokaryotes use different spatial\narrangements and sometimes different terminal electron acceptors. The\nfigure is a balance overview, not a complete reaction mechanism.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Cellular respiration couples oxidation of organic molecules to an\nelectrochemical gradient. M5-L5 examines light-driven formation of\nreduced carbon compounds.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L5<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-2-glycolysis\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 7.2 Glycolysis<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Investment and payoff phases · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-3-oxidation-of-pyruvate-and-the-citric-acid-cycle\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 7.3 Oxidation of Pyruvate and the Citric Acid Cycle<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nPyruvate oxidation, cycle products · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-4-oxidative-phosphorylation\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 7.4 Oxidative Phosphorylation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Electron transport and\nchemiosmosis · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-7-regulation-of-cellular-respiration\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 7.7 Regulation of Cellular Respiration<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Regulation of\nrespiratory pathways · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7919390/\">Chandel\n– Mitochondria (2021)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Complete oxidation of glucose and\napproximate ATP yields · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M5-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M5-L5 – Photosynthesis and energy metabolism<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Biochemistry and Bioenergetics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L2<\/a>: membranes; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L4<\/a>: chemiosmosis and redox\nreactions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Connect light reactions and the\nCalvin–Benson cycle functionally, calculate ATP and NADPH requirements\nand distinguish C3, C4 and CAM strategies.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Oxygenic photosynthesis converts radiant energy into chemical free\nenergy. It supplies both reduced carbon compounds and oxygen. The oxygen\nmolecule originates from water, not from carbon dioxide used in\nassimilation. Photosynthetically active plants also perform cellular\nrespiration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"light-reactions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Light reactions<\/h3>\n<p>In chloroplasts, pigment–protein complexes are located in thylakoid\nmembranes. Antenna pigments transfer excitation energy to reaction\ncentres. Photosystem II (PSII) removes electrons from water; transfer of\nfour electrons produces one O₂. Via plastoquinone, cytochrome b₆f and\nplastocyanin, electrons reach photosystem I (PSI). Further light\nexcitation enables transfer to ferredoxin and then via ferredoxin–NADP⁺\nreductase to NADP⁺, which is reduced to NADPH. Water splitting and\nelectron transport contribute to proton accumulation in the thylakoid\nlumen. Protons flow back to the stroma through ATP synthase, where ATP\nis formed.<\/p>\n<p>Cyclic electron transport around PSI can generate additional\nproton-motive force without directly producing NADPH or O₂. This allows\nthe ATP-to-NADPH ratio to be adjusted to metabolic demand. Chlorophyll\ndoes not absorb all wavelengths equally; energy not used in\nphotochemistry may be released as heat or fluorescence, among other\npathways.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"carbon-fixation-and-adaptation\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Carbon fixation and\nadaptation<\/h3>\n<p>In the stroma, Rubisco catalyses addition of CO₂ to\nribulose-1,5-bisphosphate (RuBP). The unstable six-carbon product splits\ninto two 3-phosphoglycerate molecules. ATP and NADPH enable reduction to\ntriose phosphates; a portion is used to regenerate RuBP. For three fixed\nCO₂, net output is one triose phosphate (3 C) at a cost of nine ATP and\nsix NADPH. Triose phosphates can be used to produce sucrose and starch,\namong other products. These reactions do not directly require light but\nin living chloroplasts are closely coupled to light reactions and\nregulation.<\/p>\n<p>Rubisco can also react with O₂. Photorespiration then requires an\nenergy-consuming recovery pathway that releases some previously fixed\ncarbon. C4 plants typically concentrate CO₂ through spatial separation\nof processes; CAM plants separate initial CO₂ uptake and subsequent\nsupply to Rubisco in time. Both strategies can provide advantages under\ncertain warm or dry conditions, but impose additional energetic\ncosts.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_027_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Functional diagram: light drives PSII and PSI; electron transport and water splitting generate a proton gradient, and NADPH and ATP supply the Calvin cycle.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Functional diagram: light drives PSII and\nPSI; electron transport and water splitting generate a proton gradient,\nand NADPH and ATP supply the Calvin cycle.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Functional diagram: light drives PSII and PSI; electron\ntransport and water splitting generate a proton gradient, and NADPH and\nATP supply the Calvin cycle.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>In the idealized Calvin cycle, net output of two triose phosphates\nrequires six CO₂, 18 ATP and 12 NADPH. Producing 12 NADPH from water by\nlinear electron transport requires 24 electrons; one O₂ is formed for\nevery four electrons, giving a balance of six O₂. This describes an\nideal coupling of the redox balance, not the measured net gas exchange\nof a leaf undergoing simultaneous respiration and photorespiration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many ATP and NADPH does the idealized Calvin cycle require for\ntwelve fixed CO₂?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In which chloroplast compartment is ATP formed during\nphotophosphorylation?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can stomatal closure reduce both water loss and CO₂\nfixation?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">36 ATP and 24 NADPH.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">On the stromal side of the thylakoid membrane, through proton flow\nback from the lumen.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Gas exchange is restricted; water vapour escapes more slowly, while\nless CO₂ can enter the leaf.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The stoichiometric figures apply to the idealized Calvin cycle\nwithout additional metabolic costs. Actual net assimilation depends on\nlight, temperature, CO₂ supply, stomatal conductance and respiration,\namong other factors. Linear electron transport is only one possible\nelectron pathway. The figure shows functional coupling, not an\natomically resolved photosystem structure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Photosynthesis and respiration both use electron transport, proton\ngradients and ATP synthases. Their energy sources and net material\nfluxes differ. M6 builds on these foundations to explore molecular\ninformation processing in cells.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L1<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-2-the-light-dependent-reactions-of-photosynthesis\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 8.2 The Light-Dependent Reactions of Photosynthesis<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nPhotosystems and electron transport · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 8.3 Using Light Energy to Make Organic Molecules<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nCalvin cycle, photorespiration and carbon fixation adaptations · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-4-oxidative-phosphorylation\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 7.4 Oxidative Phosphorylation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Electron transport and\nchemiosmosis · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
